From ef5c38f27811e90d9c70020b4a27f8f872edf452 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tal Zussman <tz2294@columbia.edu>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 14:16:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] mm, bpf: forward-port cache_ext (customizable page-cache
 eviction via eBPF)

Forward-ports cache_ext, a framework for pluggable Linux page-cache
eviction policies implemented as eBPF struct_ops programs. Adds a new
struct_ops type (page_cache_ext_ops: init/evict_folios/folio_added/
folio_accessed/folio_evicted), a per-memcg valid-folios tracking set
and a small data-structure registry (indexed lists) that eBPF eviction
policies use to rank and select folios, and hooks the eviction path in
mm/vmscan.c's shrink_lruvec() so a loaded policy gets first refusal on
the inactive file LRU before the kernel's own reclaim logic runs. Fully
opt-in and inert until an eBPF program is actually loaded and its owning
cgroup enabled via /proc/page_cache_ext_enabled_cgroup: with no policy
loaded, page_cache_ext_ops stays NULL and every hook is a no-op read of
that pointer, so it changes nothing for a system that doesn't use it.

Original design and implementation: Tal Zussman, Ioannis Zarkadas,
Jeremy Carin, Andrew Cheng, Hubertus Franke, Jonas Pfefferle, Asaf
Cidon. "cache_ext: Customizing the Page Cache with eBPF", SOSP '25.
https://doi.org/10.1145/3731569.3764820
https://github.com/cache-ext/cache_ext (GPL-2.0, artifact-evaluated)
Forward-ported from their v6.6.8 kernel fork
(https://github.com/cache-ext/linux-cache-ext) to v7.1.10. The upstream
repo's new files carried no SPDX tag; added GPL-2.0 here to match the
tree they now live in.

Forward-Port-Notes: mm/filemap.c, kernel/trace/bpf_trace.c,
 include/linux/{hashtable,btf_ids}.h applied unchanged (git apply --check
 clean against v7.1.10 as-is).

 Real v6.6.8 -> v7.1.10 API drift found and fixed, none of it cosmetic:
 - struct bpf_struct_ops: .reg/.unreg gained a `struct bpf_link *link`
   parameter (compare kernel/sched/ext.c's bpf_scx_reg/bpf_scx_unreg);
   .cfi_stubs is now load-bearing, not optional -- omitting it makes
   bpf_struct_ops_desc_init() return -EINVAL at registration time with
   only a pr_warn to say why. Added __bpf_ops_page_cache_ext_ops, a
   static instance of struct page_cache_ext_ops populated with trivial
   stub functions (never called; only their addresses are read), same
   pattern as kernel/sched/ext.c's __bpf_ops_sched_ext_ops. Added
   .owner = THIS_MODULE, also now required.
 - kernel/bpf/bpf_struct_ops_types.h and its BPF_STRUCT_OPS_TYPE(...)
   X-macro registration list are gone entirely on this kernel, replaced
   by an explicit register_bpf_struct_ops(&ops, type) call per subsystem
   (see net/ipv4/bpf_tcp_ca.c's bpf_tcp_ca_kfunc_init). Nothing else
   ever called this for page_cache_ext_ops -- added
   bpf_page_cache_ext_kfunc_init() as a late_initcall in
   mm/page_cache_ext.c. Without it the type builds fine and silently
   never becomes available to any BPF program; there is no error at
   build or load time pointing at a missing X-macro entry that no
   longer exists to be missing from.
 - bpf_base_func_proto() gained a `const struct bpf_prog *prog` second
   argument.
 - mm/vmscan.c: lruvec locking helper renamed unlock_page_lruvec_irq()
   -> lruvec_unlock_irq().
 - kernel/bpf/verifier.c: the old is_callback_calling_kfunc() single
   function was split into is_sync_callback_calling_kfunc() /
   is_async_callback_calling_kfunc(); cache_ext's two new
   callback-calling kfuncs (list iterate/sample) are sync, so they're
   wired into is_sync_callback_calling_kfunc() alongside
   is_bpf_rbtree_add_kfunc(), not the now-gone original function. The
   callback-state-setter dispatch site similarly moved from
   __check_func_call() to push_callback_call(). struct
   bpf_retval_range's minval/maxval are s32 (bpf_verifier.h), not s64 --
   the sample callback's tracked return range is retval_range(S32_MIN,
   S32_MAX) rather than the wider range the original targeted; the real
   eBPF calling convention is unaffected (r0 is always 64-bit), only the
   verifier's static range-tracking precision for this one callback.
 - include/linux/mm_types.h, include/linux/memcontrol.h: pure struct
   additions re-anchored past two years of unrelated struct-layout
   churn (mem_cgroup_per_node's cacheline-padding restructure, the
   CONFIG_MEMCG_V1 split into memcontrol-v1.c, struct mem_cgroup's
   nodeinfo[] flexible-array-member requiring the new bool field to sit
   immediately before it, not after).
 - mm/readahead.c: the new prefetch tracepoint's #include goes after
   <trace/events/readahead.h>, with CREATE_TRACE_POINTS undefined again
   first -- the same shape mm/memcontrol.c already uses to pull in
   vmscan.h after defining memcg.h's tracepoints. define_trace.h clears
   CREATE_TRACE_POINTS only for its own recursive re-read and restores
   it before it returns, so leaving it set makes readahead.o emit a
   second copy of every tracepoint in filemap.h, not just the new one.
   Each object still compiles clean on its own; vmlinux.o is where it
   shows up, as 40 duplicate symbols against mm/filemap.o. Moving the
   #include above the CREATE_TRACE_POINTS block instead does not work
   either -- that breaks the block's own macro expansion
   (STATIC_CALL_TRAMP/check_trace_callback_type undeclared errors).
 - Dropped, deliberately: the legacy cgroup-v1 page_tracking.nr_entries
   cftype entry (that whole interface split out to mm/memcontrol-v1.c;
   the cgroup-v2 default equivalent is kept) and one unconditional
   pr_info_ratelimited() the upstream diff added to the verifier's
   backtracking hot path with no apparent connection to cache_ext's own
   feature -- looked like incidental dev-branch noise, not a
   correctness or feature requirement.
 - Removed mm/page_cache_ext_ds.h: shipped empty (0 bytes) in upstream
   and #include'd by nothing; the declarations it once presumably held
   live in include/linux/cache_ext.h instead.

 Verified: every touched/added object (mm/{filemap,memcontrol,readahead,
 swap,vmscan,page_cache_ext,page_cache_ext_ds}.o,
 kernel/bpf/verifier.o, kernel/trace/bpf_trace.o) compiles clean against
 this tree's real .config. Not yet booted; no eviction policy loaded or
 measured against a real workload on this hardware -- both are follow-up
 work before this leaves the "compiles" stage of evidence, per this
 project's own standing rule that a patch needs a real before/after
 measurement, not a compile, to be considered proven.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-sonnet-5

Follow-up fix (found by a real build, not review): the original port put
struct cache_ext_ds_registry (which embeds a DECLARE_HASHTABLE) directly
into struct mem_cgroup_per_node, and put valid_folios_set/valid_folio's
full definitions plus #include <linux/hashtable.h> straight into
include/linux/memcontrol.h. That header is transitively included by close
to the whole kernel (security.h -> bpf.h -> memcontrol.h reaches
kernel/trace/ftrace.c, among many others), so hashtable.h's hash_add()
macro became visible in every one of those translation units -- including
kernel/trace/ftrace.c, which has its own unrelated static hash_add()
function. Real build failure: "macro 'hash_add' requires 3 arguments, but
only 2 given" at kernel/trace/ftrace.c:6312, caught only because a full
make bindeb-pkg got to 34000/34000 objects before hitting it -- an
object-level compile of just the touched files never would have.

Fixed at the root, not by renaming ftrace.c's unrelated static function:
moved the full struct valid_folios_set/valid_folio definitions into
include/linux/cache_ext.h (which already needs hashtable.h for its own
cache_ext_ds_registry), changed mem_cgroup_per_node's
cache_ext_ds_registry field from embedded-by-value to a pointer (matching
how valid_folios_set was already a pointer), and left memcontrol.h with
plain forward declarations. memcontrol.c and page_cache_ext.c each gained
their own direct #include <linux/cache_ext.h> where they actually
dereference these types; ftrace.c and the rest of the kernel no longer see
hashtable.h through memcontrol.h at all. Re-verified: every touched
object recompiles clean, including kernel/trace/ftrace.o, and the full
20-patch series still re-applies via a real sequential git am --3way
against a fresh base-7.1.10 worktree with zero conflicts.

Second follow-up fix (found by booting it, which is the only thing that
could have found it): the port kept upstream's BTF_SET8_START/END around
both kfunc sets in mm/page_cache_ext_ds.c. On v6.6.8 that was the kfunc
macro. It is not one any more. include/linux/btf_ids.h now defines
BTF_SET8_START(name) as __BTF_SET8_START(name, local, 0) -- flags zero --
and BTF_KFUNCS_START(name) as __BTF_SET8_START(name, local,
BTF_SET8_KFUNCS). register_btf_kfunc_id_set() rejects a set without that
flag:

	if (!(kset->set->flags & BTF_SET8_KFUNCS)) {
		WARN_ON(!kset->owner);
		return -EINVAL;
	}

so every boot of the ported kernel threw a WARNING at
kernel/bpf/btf.c:9004 out of register_cache_ext_kfuncs() and then
"cache_ext: failed to register kfunc sets (-22)". kset->owner is
THIS_MODULE, NULL for built-in code, which is what turns the -EINVAL into
a splat as well. All eight cache_ext kfuncs were absent from the
verifier's kfunc table, so the struct_ops type registered and attached
fine while any policy that actually called a kfunc would have been
rejected at load. Switched both sets to BTF_KFUNCS_START/END, which is
what all ~40 in-tree kfunc sites use.

Nothing else needed changing: __bpf_kfunc is only __used __retain
__noclone noinline (include/linux/btf.h), with no BTF decl-tag
dependency, and btf_check_kfunc_protos() wants FUNC -> FUNC_PROTO in
vmlinux BTF, which those attributes already guarantee.

Worth recording why this survived a verification pass: the runtime test
checked that /proc/page_cache_ext_enabled_cgroup existed and that
bpf_struct_ops_page_cache_ext_ops and its ops were in vmlinux BTF. Both
were true. The BPF probe it loaded called no cache_ext kfunc at all, so
the one broken thing was the one thing not exercised.
